#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/id #uuid "c375d3cc-e398-5a47-a69f-ddc853431752" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/id #uuid "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13009" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=4 \\\\ x+2y &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =4 \\\\ x & =4+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tx+2y &=16 \\\\\n\t(4+y) + 2y &=16\\\\\n\t4+3y &=16\\\\\n\t3y &=12\\\\\n\ty &=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into $x=4+y$, then we can find the value of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tx &= 4 + y \\\\\n\t   &= 4 + (4) \\\\\n\t   &=8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073446 :question/id #uuid "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224" :question/math-academy-id "e-13010" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2u+9v-6 &=0 \\\\ 2u+6v-8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n\n$$\n\\qquad\\:\\:\\: \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}2u+9v-6} &{\\color{red}{}=0} && {\\color{red}(1)} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2u+6v-8} &{\\color{blue}{}=0} && {\\color{blue}(2)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\\\n3v &= -2 \\\\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\\\n\tu &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 487 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186